# Flags the CLI accepts that are NOT overrides -- the complement of
# docs/waivers.md. Together the two must account for EVERY flag the parser
# accepts on any verb, so a new flag of any shape fails the sweep until
# somebody has decided which side it is on.
#
# Format:  <carrier>:<name>  <why it does not lift a refusal>.  Carriers:
# flag: a CLI flag | env: an environment name | file: a .truth/ policy file.
# The key is NAMESPACED because the same name can be a flag on one carrier
# and nothing on another, and because a bare key cannot say which list it
# is being excused from.  ONE ENTRY PER LINE;
# there are no continuation lines, because a parser that reads `flag =
# everything up to the first space` would read an indented continuation as
# a flag named after its first word.
#
# A REASON IS REQUIRED, and it must be a FINISHED SENTENCE: one ending in a
# conjunction or a preposition, or with no sentence-final mark, is a
# finding. Three entries here were truncated mid-clause on the first pass
# -- `--basis`, `--watch-policy` and `--ttl-days` -- and a half-written
# reason is worse than a missing one, because it reads as a judgement that
# somebody made rather than one that was never finished.
#
# Scoping this file by NAME is what it replaces. Three real overrides have
# no `-ok` suffix: --refresh-evidence (takes a sentence), --single-run
# (takes nothing and leaves no trace in the record at all) and --ttl-days
# (takes a number, and an explicit one suppresses ADR-032's decay).

# --- output and machine-readability -------------------------------------
flag:--json  selects machine output; it changes no gate and files nothing.
flag:--ready-json  selects the tracker's JSON shape; a rendering choice only.
flag:--stdin  takes the tracker feed from stdin rather than from a command, which changes where input comes from and nothing about what is admitted.
flag:--live  filters the listing to live claims; read-only.
flag:--stale  filters the listing to stale claims; read-only.
flag:--diverged  filters the listing to diverged claims; read-only.
flag:--retracted  filters the listing to retracted claims; read-only.
flag:--disputed  filters the listing to disputed claims; read-only.
flag:--unverified  filters the listing to unverified claims; read-only.
flag:--cannot-verify  filters the listing to cannot_verify claims; read-only.
flag:--since  narrows an event window; read-only.
flag:--under  narrows a report to one path prefix; read-only.
flag:--exclude  narrows a sweep by pattern; read-only.
flag:--inverse  flips `impact` to ask which files no claim watches; read-only.
flag:--arm  filters `reproduce` to one capsule outcome; read-only.

# --- the content of a filing, not permission to make it -----------------
flag:--text  the claim sentence itself, which is the thing judged rather than a judgement.
flag:--basis  the reasoning basis, and REQUIRED for an INFERRED claim, which makes it the opposite of a waiver: it is demanded, never granted.
flag:--claim  the claim-at-death sentence on `done`; content, and required.
flag:--claim-basis  that claim's own basis; required, so it excuses nothing.
flag:--evidence-cmd  the command whose output becomes the evidence; the screen that may refuse it is lifted by --evidence-unsafe-ok, never by this.
flag:--accept-cmd  the acceptance oracle's command; the screen that may refuse it is lifted by --accept-unsafe-ok, never by this.
flag:--paths  the watch set itself; the budget it may exceed is lifted by --paths-ok, never by this. The `<path>#<selector>` exemption from those budgets (ADR-055) is carried by PATH SYNTAX rather than by this flag, and it now has its own row in docs/waivers.md under carrier `syntax`.
flag:--watch-policy  names a watch set reviewed once and reused (FAZA 3); choosing it means the freehand budget is never reached, which is the opposite of lifting it.
flag:--tier  the cost tier; content.
flag:--class  the evidence class; content.
flag:--deps  issue dependencies; content.
flag:--premise  an issue premise; content.
flag:--successor  the id a retraction points at, REQUIRED by `--cause restated`; demanded rather than granted.
flag:--supersedes  the dead premise this link replaces; ADR-013 REFUSES it while the old premise still passes ready, so a refusal acts on it rather than through it.
flag:--cause  ADR-049's retraction cause {restated,expired,wrong}, REQUIRED on every retraction; demanded rather than granted.
flag:--accept-kind  the oracle's kind {verification,validation}; content.

# --- verb selection and mode, not gate lifting --------------------------
flag:--cancel  selects the cancel form of `done`; it chooses a transition.
flag:--reopen  selects the reopen transition; it chooses a transition.
flag:--release  selects the release form of the tag check, which is the stricter form rather than a relaxation.
flag:--recheck  re-executes a recorded capsule; it can only ADD a refusal, never remove one.
flag:--reproduce  re-executes without filing; read-only.
flag:--diff  folds a second ref and prints the delta; read-only.
flag:--mechanical  ADR-012 display subtype on a diverge; it never changes status.
flag:--append-order  selects append order for a batch read; read-only.

# --- environment names that are not overrides ---------------------------
env:TRUTH_ACTOR  records who filed; identity for attribution, and it lifts nothing on its own.
env:TRUTH_TRACKER_CMD  names the tracker command `ready` reads; it selects a source rather than admitting a filing.
env:TRUTH_BD_CMD  names the command the bd adapter shells out to; tool selection.
env:TRUTH_MUTMUT_SUITE  names the suite mutation scoring runs against; tool selection, and it gates nothing at intake or push.
env:USER  the fallback for TRUTH_ACTOR when it is unset; identity, not permission.
env:BATTERY  an INHERITED input to the battery's own gate: it points the arms at a substitute battery so they can be proved against a deliberately broken one. It selects what is under test rather than lifting a gate, and the gate it feeds refuses on its own terms either way.
env:STUB_CAUGHT  an INHERITED input to one arm of the battery gate, passed to a child by `env` on the line that sets it; it seeds that arm's fixture and admits no filing.

# --- .truth/ files that are not waiver carriers -------------------------
file:.truth/claims.jsonl  the ledger itself; it is the thing gates judge, not a policy that excuses them, and the CLI is the only writer.
file:.truth/watch-policies  names reviewed watch sets; a named policy is the reviewed alternative to a freehand set, so it narrows rather than excuses.
file:.truth/evidence-deny  the ADR-022 deny list; adding a line REFUSES more, never less.
file:.truth/retracted-figures  the retracted-figure sweep's input; a line there makes the sweep refuse MORE occurrences, not fewer.
file:.truth/arm-index-link-hashes  a recorded hash, not an excuse; a stale entry makes the sweep report SUSPECT rather than silent.
file:.truth/arm-index-prose-hashes  a recorded hash, not an excuse; the same reasoning as the link hashes.

# --- environment names that exist only as TEST DATA ---------------------
# The harvest is syntactic: a test that WRITES an env read as a string
# literal is indistinguishable from code that performs one. It
# over-reports rather than under-reports, which is the direction a safety
# reading must fail in, and the residue is classified here by hand.
env:TRUTH_NEW_ESCAPE  a literal inside a test fixture in template/scripts/test-integrations.py, never read by any code path.
env:TRUTH_ENTRYPOINT_BYPASS  a literal inside the same fixture, in the arm that proves the harvest reaches an extensionless Python entry point.
